@page "/server"
@inject HttpClient Http

<PageTitle>Server round-trip</PageTitle>
<h1>Server round-trip</h1>
<p>This form deliberately skips client-side submit validation — press Send and watch the server's 400 land on the exact fields. Press "Send to server" rather than Enter — Enter triggers the browser's implicit form submission, which runs the client-side submit pipeline this page deliberately skips.</p>
@if (IsHostedDemo)
{
    <p><small>This hosted demo has no server behind it — an in-browser handler answers with the same validators and the same response shapes the real API would send.</small></p>
}

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "RoundTripOrder.cs", "ServerRoundTrip.razor", "ServerRoundTrip.razor.cs", "OrdersController.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li>This form is judged by the <strong>server</strong>: the client submit pipeline is deliberately skipped, and the API's 400 response is applied to the exact fields it names.</li>
            <li>Each response <em>replaces</em> the previous server verdict — a corrected resubmission can never leave stale errors behind.</li>
            <li>The verdict applies at the severity it carries: the response's errors block and mark their fields <code>formidable-invalid</code>, while its advisories land on the fields they name. One call carries both — the page writes no advisory plumbing of its own.</li>
            <li>Before sending, the page calls <code>Normalize()</code> — whitespace-only lines are dropped on both sides, so the server's line numbers always match the rows you see.</li>
            <li>The same validation filter runs in both hosting styles — pick Minimal API or the MVC controller above and the 400 shape is identical.</li>
            <li><code>OrdersController.cs</code> in the panel below is real server source — the MVC twin of the minimal-API mapping, behind the same filter. <code>requests.http</code> in the API project calls both endpoints without the UI.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            @if (IsHostedDemo)
            {
                <li>No server to start on this hosted demo — the in-browser handler answers as soon as you click Send.</li>
            }
            else
            {
                <li>Start the API first: <code>dotnet run --project samples/Formidable.Sample.Api</code>.</li>
            }
            <li>Click <em>Send to server</em> with everything empty — the 400 lands inline on the exact fields.</li>
            <li>Click an entry in the summary — it focuses that field.</li>
            <li>Fix one field and send again — the new verdict replaces the old one entirely.</li>
            <li>Put a hyphen in the description while a SKU is still empty and send — the 400 carries the server's advisory about the hyphen alongside the SKU error, and it lands on the description as a warning that blocks nothing.</li>
            <li>Complete the order and send — the server accepts and the status line confirms.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<fieldset>
    <legend>Endpoint</legend>
    <label><input type="radio" name="endpoint" checked='@(_endpoint == "/api/orders/")' @onchange='() => _endpoint = "/api/orders/"' /> Minimal API</label>
    <label><input type="radio" name="endpoint" checked='@(_endpoint == "/api/controller/orders")' @onchange='() => _endpoint = "/api/controller/orders"' /> MVC controller</label>
</fieldset>

<p class="endpoint-caption"><small>POST <code>@_endpoint</code> on the API (port 5180)</small></p>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_order">
    <FormidableSummary />

    <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_order.Description" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _order.Description" /></div>

    @foreach (var line in _order.Lines)
    {
        <div class="field" @key="line"><label>SKU <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="line.Sku" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => line.Sku" /></div>
    }

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="button" @onclick="() => _order.Lines.Add(new OrderLine())">Add line</button>
        <button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Send">Send to server</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
